\section{Results}

\subsection{Encrypted RMHD Performance Under Supersonic Shock Conditions}
To evaluate the fidelity of encrypted RMHD evolution, ADLE‑v1 was subjected to a
Mach $M_s = 15.5$ shock propagating through a dense molecular medium.
Encrypted density and velocity fields reproduced the expected Rankine–Hugoniot
jump conditions with deviations below $10^{-4}$ relative error when compared to
plaintext reference simulations. Linear advection operators, implemented via
ciphertext rotations, exhibited no measurable scale drift across the full
modulus chain.

\subsection{Chemical Network Accuracy Across Extreme Thermal Gradients}
The astrochemical subsystem was tested using a 450-species and a 1200-species
reaction network spanning temperatures from $5\,\mathrm{K}$ to $5\times10^{4}\,\mathrm{K}$.
Chebyshev polynomial approximations of degree $d=3$ and $d=5$ maintained
chemical fidelity with maximum deviation below $2\%$ for stiff ion–molecule
channels. Encrypted polynomial evaluation consumed between 2.0 and 3.5
multiplicative levels per timestep, consistent with theoretical depth
predictions.

\subsection{Lorentz Tensor Product Evaluation}
Fully encrypted tensor products for the Lorentz force term,
$\mathbf{J}\times\mathbf{B}$, were benchmarked under high-field conditions.
Encrypted–encrypted multiplications increased noise by approximately
$2^{8}$--$2^{10}$ per operation, but rescaling preserved numerical stability.
Magnetic tension and pressure contributions matched plaintext RMHD outputs
within $10^{-3}$ relative error.

\subsection{Noise Growth and Stability Envelope Validation}
Noise evolution across sequential timesteps $k$ followed the predicted relation
\begin{equation}
\|\mathbf{e}_k\|_\infty \approx \|\mathbf{e}_0\|_\infty
+ k\,C_{\text{net}}\,\Delta^2 N,
\end{equation}
with empirical measurements agreeing to within $5\%$.
Under Tier~3 hyper-stiff conditions, the ciphertext noise reached the safety
threshold $q_4/16$ at timestep $k \approx 42$, confirming the theoretical
encrypted stability envelope. No decryption failures or wrap-around events were
observed prior to bootstrap activation.

\subsection{Bootstrap Refresh Performance}
The asynchronous CKKS bootstrap pipeline restored ciphertext modulus height
from $q_4$ to $q_0$ while preserving scale alignment across all fields.
Post-bootstrap RMHD and chemical evolution resumed without numerical artifacts.
The refresh latency remained constant across all tests, demonstrating that
ADLE‑v1 can sustain arbitrarily long encrypted simulations.

\subsection{Comparison With Plaintext RMHD Codes}
Encrypted RMHD evolution was compared against a plaintext reference solver
using identical initial conditions. Across all test regimes—supersonic shocks,
hyper-stiff chemistry, and strong-field Lorentz coupling—ADLE‑v1 maintained
agreement within $10^{-3}$ to $10^{-4}$ relative error. These results confirm
that homomorphic evaluation preserves the physical correctness of RMHD and
astrochemical dynamics.